hi  this is dr gregory sadler i'm a  professor of philosophy  and the president and founder of an  educational consulting company called  reason io  where we put philosophy into practice  i've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years  and i find that many people run into  difficulties reading classic  philosophical texts  sometimes it's the way things are said  or how the text is structured  but the concepts themselves are not  always that complicated and that's where  i come in  to help students and lifelong learners  i've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube  many viewers say they find them useful  what you're currently watching is part  of a new series of shorter videos  each of them focused on one core concept  from an important philosophical text  i hope you find it useful as well  in the conclusion to tom's  summary of gilligan's ethics of care and  some of the criticisms that have been  made  in response to those criticisms she says  that  we don't want to try to you know rule  traditional caring roles out all  together  the world like she said it would be a  much worse place tomorrow than there  were today if  women were suddenly to stop meeting the  physical and psychological needs of  those  who depend on them just because men and  yes children have more or less routinely  taken advantage of some women's  willingness to serve them does not mean  that every woman's  caring action should be contemptuously  dismissed as yet another instance of  pathological masochism fear success and  passivity  so she says care is worth rescuing care  is a value  is worth rescuing from these these you  know  ways of domination she calls them  patriarchal structures we can think of  as  historical ways in which women have  gotten the short end of the stick  and sometimes still do get the short end  of the stick  in less in some parts of our culture but  equal in some other parts  so she says a person cannot truly care  for someone  if they're forced to do so and what  would that compulsion be  if they're economically forced to care  for someone so if you're stuck in a  situation where  the only way that you can actually stay  alive or you know put food on the table  is to care for another person  it's a bit coercive it's not something  that you can choose to give  socially if it's just a matter of social  expectations you know very traditional  communities  or psychologically somebody's coercing  that person psychologically  so a person cannot truly care if they're  forced to do so  only under conditions she says of sexual  equality and freedom can women care for  men  in any without any way diminishing  disempowering and or disregarding them  so until these conditions are achieved  that's that's kind of a utopian  view until these are cheap we want to  have some criteria  for what care ought to look like so  she says care has to  fulfill the one  that is caring so it has to be something  that the person who is engaging in the  care  can say yes this this is important to me  this is satisfying to me  this is something that i can willingly  choose and endorse  as part of my good the way i want to be  the kind of person i want to be  that would rule out a lot of cases where  somebody just has to care because  they're the one who's you know is that  their role or it's expected of them  this is really interesting she says  calls upon  the unique  and particular  so if i am caring for  my daughter  there's no substitute for my caring for  my daughter  somebody else could care for her but it  would not be the same  care you know i die  she gets some step dad somewhere down  the line or some father figure or  something like that  that's good it's i would love for her to  be cared for by somebody else  but it won't be my individuality  that'll do that there's certain things  that i've shared with her  for example i used to take her fishing  you know or we would go out  and blaze trails um through through the  woods  we you know certainly like i remember  one time it was kind of silly to bring  it up but  it was you know machete and i was  hacking away and stuff  and i cut my thumb she got real worried  because i started to bleed a lot  you know and i stopped the bleeding and  i  you know don't tell anyone we had a  little secret between us  you know but that's that's how care  takes place and those  those are things are part of our  individuality in particular  particularly what you've shared with  your parents  what you're going to share with people  you care for  when you're carrying that that matters  that's why you can't be substituted for  so that's one important criteria the  third important criteria  is not produced by a person in a role  because of gender with one gender  engaging in nurturing behavior the other  engaging in instrumental behavior  so it's not based  on gender  roles so the guy doesn't get to  automatically assume  i'm going to show up and and get cared  for because  i'm the guy and there's certain ways in  which  women also traditionally expect caring  to take place  that this would also rule out as well  the  i'm not going to work you're just  supposed to take care of me sort of  sort of thing financially that would  rule that out as well  but for the most part she's concerned  about men  coming in and saying you know where's my  dinner  right that's sort of stereotypical  type of type of stuff here's a very  that extends it a bit further  this won't be possible at a certain  sense  genuine care is reciprocated by the  other person  caring in return this will only be  possible in some circumstances though  right if you're caring for somebody  who's an invalid  they probably won't be able to give you  the level of care  that you're giving them obviously caring  for children  is you know dealing with very egoistic  beings for a long long time who are not  that respectful  of your wants and needs or desires or  anything like that so parents eventually  come up and say get that  out of here i just want some time for  myself you know  but in say in adult relationships think  about a friendship for example  if you care about your friend that  friend  want to be caring about you in return  right  otherwise it's kind of a one-way  relationship and you're being taken  advantage you're being taken advantage  of  in your your caring and this does happen  to a lot of people  um then her fifth thing  this might be a bit optional depending  on what you want to make of it  takes place within  the framework  of consciousness  raising  now you know when you talk about that it  sounds like a uh  very academic kind of class thing or  activism doesn't it  like uh engaging consciousness raising  kind of  actually reminds me of the hippies for  my age  i don't know what it reminds you guys of  it but  you hear this sort of stuff all the time  it doesn't have to be like that what do  they mean by consciousness raising  becoming aware of things becoming aware  of what's going on  you know so for example getting into an  adult relationship  let's say it's a romantic relationship  where you and your partner  equally care about each other could help  you to actually understand  how your parents behave towards each  other and whether it was good or bad  that would be an example of  consciousness raising  it helps you understand things better i  think she means that in a very political  sense  you probably don't necessarily need to  to push it that that way  but if you like that sort of thing that  you know  and you know get together i guess once a  week and  talk about how your relationship is  helping you understand politics i don't  think that would be a bad thing but  i think this could be more about our own  past our narratives where we're going  that sort of thing so what do you think  is this enough to sort of guarantee  that care  is going to  be exercised rightly will this keep you  on the tracks  or not do you think there's anything  left out  any criteria that need to be in there  that aren't in there  this is kind of a work in processes  ethics of care stuff  so it's good to think about that

